// scrml.dev /reference/errors/E-DERIVED-WRITE
//
// Reference page for E-DERIVED-WRITE. Assignment to a derived reactive value.
//
// SPEC anchors: §6.6.8, §6.6.16, §6.6.17, §6.6.18, §34.
//
// Written 2026-07-26. Prose derived from the compiler's own emission message in
// compiler/src; the reproducer below is the exact source compiled against the
// linked compiler to verify what this code does.

<page>

    // REFERENCE SIDEBAR opt-in — static, page-scoped, zero JS. The nav is
    // authored once in app.scrml and hidden by default; this shows it and
    // marks THIS page's link active. See scripts/gen-reference-nav.mjs.
    #{
        /* !important is load-order, not laziness: the shell's app.css is linked
           AFTER this page's stylesheet, so its `.refnav{display:none}` default
           wins at equal specificity. */
        .ref-shell { display: grid !important; }
        .refnav { display: block !important; }
        .refnav-link[data-ref="errors/E-DERIVED-WRITE"] {
            color: rgb(125 211 252);
            background-color: rgb(14 165 233 / 0.10);
            border-left-color: rgb(56 189 248);
            font-weight: 600;
        }
    }

    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-3xl">

        <header class="not-prose mb-8 pb-6 border-b border-slate-200">
            <div class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-500 mb-2">
                Reference &rsaquo; Errors
            </div>
            <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-rose-700 font-mono">
                E-DERIVED-WRITE
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 mt-2">
                Assignment to a derived reactive value.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-4 text-xs">
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-rose-50 text-rose-700 rounded font-semibold">
                    Error
                </span>
                <span class="px-2 py-1 bg-slate-100 text-slate-700 rounded">
                    Compile-time
                </span>
                <a href="https://github.com/bryanmaclee/scrml/blob/main/compiler/SPEC.md#6"
                   class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                    SPEC §6.6.8 (normative)
                </a>
            </div>
        </header>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            What it means
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            A derived cell (<code>const &lt;name&gt; = expr</code>) is defined entirely by its expression. Its value is recomputed whenever a dependency changes, so any value you assign to it directly would be discarded at the next recomputation. Rather than let that silently happen, the compiler rejects the write. The same code also fires for <code>default=</code> on a derived cell, which is the declaration-time form of the same mistake.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Minimal reproducer
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            This is the exact source compiled against the linked compiler and verified to produce <code>E-DERIVED-WRITE</code>.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>&lt;program title="p"&gt;
    &lt;count&gt; = 1
    const &lt;double&gt; = @count * 2
    function bump() &#123; @double = 9 &#125;
    &lt;button onclick=bump()&gt;go&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/&gt;</code></pre>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            How to fix
        </h2>

        <ol class="space-y-3 text-slate-700 list-decimal list-outside ml-6">
            <li>
                <strong>Write the <em>source</em> cell instead.</strong>
                Assign to whichever plain cell the derived expression reads. The derived value updates on its own — that is the entire point of declaring it derived.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Make it a plain cell if it genuinely needs writing.</strong>
                If a value is sometimes computed and sometimes set by hand, it is not derived. Declare it as <code>&lt;name&gt; = init</code> and compute it explicitly.
            </li>
        </ol>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-10 mb-4">
            Related
        </h2>

        <ul class="space-y-1 text-slate-700">
            <li>
